WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.003
How does the nose keep a thousand receptor types on separate lines?

2
00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:13.057
Most mature olfactory neurons in a mouse express one receptor gene at high

3
00:00:13.057 --> 00:00:16.717
abundance. That receptor can still bind many odorants.

4
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.690
So all the neurons with one receptor form one line.

5
00:00:21.683 --> 00:00:23.143
Where do their axons go?

6
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:28.623
Mombaerts and colleagues made one receptor's neurons fill their axons with a marker.

7
00:00:29.717 --> 00:00:32.837
The labelled cells were scattered across the epithelium.

8
00:00:33.617 --> 00:00:37.317
Their axons gathered and ended in two fixed glomeruli in each bulb.

9
00:00:38.317 --> 00:00:42.597
Each glomerulus collects mainly from neurons with one receptor type.

10
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.610
So the glomerular layer is largely a map of lines.

11
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:48.973
What does each line detect?

12
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:53.953
Malnic and colleagues imaged single mouse neurons as odorants were applied.

13
00:00:54.983 --> 00:00:58.523
Then they identified the receptor in each responding cell.

14
00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:02.157
One receptor recognized several odorants.

15
00:01:02.997 --> 00:01:05.677
One odorant was recognized by several receptors.

16
00:01:06.817 --> 00:01:11.037
And concentration could change which receptors an odorant activated.

17
00:01:12.017 --> 00:01:14.197
Together, these make a combinatorial code.

18
00:01:15.167 --> 00:01:17.067
Now practise reading such a code.

19
00:01:17.927 --> 00:01:20.047
This matrix is invented for illustration.

20
00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:24.217
Four receptors, A to D, each simply on or off.

21
00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:31.650
Odorant one turns on A and B. Odorant two, B and C. Odorant three, A, B and C.

22
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.490
Odorant four, D alone.

23
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:38.693
Remove receptor A. Which two odorants now give the same pattern?

24
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:46.013
Odorants two and three.

25
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:50.813
Both now read B and C, so the model predicts they cannot be told apart.

26
00:01:51.817 --> 00:01:56.157
Now put receptor A back, and raise odorant one to tenfold concentration.

27
00:01:57.277 --> 00:02:01.597
Receptor C has a lower affinity for it, so it responds only at the higher

28
00:02:01.597 --> 00:02:02.597
concentration.

29
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.260
What happens to odorant one's pattern?

30
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:16.960
C is recruited. Odorant one now reads A, B and C, the pattern of odorant three.

31
00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:21.050
The model predicts it could be mistaken for odorant three.

32
00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.630
Quality can change with concentration.

33
00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:27.040
This toy counts patterns, not smells.

34
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.460
What a mouse can tell apart needs a behavioural test.

35
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:33.133
Smell keeps its lines apart.

36
00:02:33.893 --> 00:02:36.613
So an odour is read as a pattern across many of them.
